WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.180
Good day everyone. This is Paul
and you are watching the Langfocus channel.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.000
And today we're going to talk 
about some interesting stuff.

00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:18.540
We're going to talk about the difference
between a language and dialect.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:19.700
Now what is the difference?

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.360
All over the world, there are people who speak 
closely related forms of communication.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:29.360
Sometimes they are called different languages. 
Sometimes they are called dialects of one language.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.620
So, how do we decide which one they are?

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.920
Sometimes there's some disagreement about 
which one a particular form of communication is.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.240
Is it a dialect or is that a separate language?

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:46.660
A dialect really means a variety of speech that 
differs somewhat from another variety of speech
of that same language.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:53.020
It could differ in grammar. It could differ in phonology 
or pronunciation. It could differ in vocabulary.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.800
It could differ in all of those maybe.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:00.520
Sometimes, those dialects are based in different 
regions.  They are regional varieties of a language.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.240
Sometimes they are ethnic varieties of a language.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.080
For example, maybe there are two ethnic groups living 
in a city. But historically, they've lived separate

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.080
and not really interacted with each other
 because of racism or whatever.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.340
And they've developed a separate dialect
because of that separation.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.620
So they're almost living in two worlds within 
the same city, that kind of thing. So...

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:25.140
It's almost like a different regional dialect inside 
your own town if you know what I mean.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.760
Now, within every language, there is some degree 
of variety, some degree of difference.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.020
There are some different spoken varieties
of every language.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.780
So how do we decide if it's close enough to the language 
to be considered a dialect? And when is it not?

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:45.120
One of the main criteria that is most 
often used is mutual intelligibility.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.980
That means the speakers of those two different 
varieties of the language can understand each other,

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:56.320
fully or almost fully understand each other. 
We would say they're mutually intelligible in that case.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:01.320
So, according to this definition, simply if you can understand each other, you can understand them,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.260
you can make yourself understood to them, 
then you are speaking the same language.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:10.780
And if even if there's some degree of difference
in grammar or phonology or vocabulary,

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.740
those are dialectal differences 
because you can understand each other.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:20.120
The languages are close enough that they are considered 
the same language, according to that definition.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.880
Now the problem with this definition is
that sometimes speakers of

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.500
what is considered to be one language
often can't understand each other.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.460
Like, for example, often, I can't understand people 
who speak a Scottish dialects of English.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:33.800
[a bell is ringing]

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:48.560
[ununderstandable scottish speech part]

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.740
Often I can't understand people who
speak a Scottish dialects of English.

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.940
Or sometimes an Australian dialect of English if
they come from like the deep countryside of Australia,

00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.620
sometimes I can't understand them.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.620
And this is especially true if I over-hear
two of those people speaking together.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.740
I hear them speaking with each other not directly to me,
then they'll speak in their full on different accent,

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.120
different dialect and sometimes I can't understand.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.420
So are we speaking a different language,
 because it's not mutually intelligible?

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:19.360
So that's kind of the difficult point of this definition.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.480
Sometimes I can't understand 
other speakers of English. But...

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.780
I think it's the same language and so do they. 
So... what's going on there?

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:32.220
So that brings me to another criteria of what would make 
two spoken varieties just dialects of one language.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.000
And that is this :

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:37.820
both of those varieties use the same
written standard variety of the language.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.500
They use the same written standard, 
the same written language

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.620
Even though the spoken language may differ somewhat.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:50.220
So that would explain why I speak English 
and a Scottish person speaks English, 
even if there may be some trouble communicating

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.780
We both use the same written language. We both 
write in the same way. We read the same books.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:01.160
And then when we speak to each other, we can adapt 
our speech to make it a little bit more like the written standard.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.920
Now that's why we can communicate, even though 
I can't really understand their variety of English,

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.580
when they speak the pure form, 
when they speak it with each other.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.780
I don't really know what they're saying a lot of the time

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.560
But when they speak to me, they kind of
use book-English a little bit, literary English.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:19.760
They use more of the written standard
and mix it in with their natural dialect,

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.680
to make it understandable to me 
and I think I do the same unconsciously.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:30.740
That's what makes us speakers of the same language, 
the fact that we... blend our variety of speech 
with that same written standard.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:33.440
And that's what helps us to communicate,
across the dialects.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:41.540
Now, the one weakness in this criterion is that 
there are  people who can't really speak 
to the standard language, that well.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.360
So that's true in English: you have some people who are 
uneducated, maybe the poor, maybe highschool dropouts,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:50.680
maybe the type of people who just don't really care 
about studying or speaking properly.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.420
They don't really know the standard language; they 
might understand it but they can't speak like that. So...

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:01.760
Are those speakers of the same language 
even though they don't really know how to use 
the standard written language properly?

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:07.680
So that's kind of the trouble with that criterion. 
But, so far, that's the best criterion that I've found

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:13.580
to explain what is a dialect and what's a language: 
that written common standard language.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:19.180
Now, as with all things in life, the situation gets 
just messy when we bring politics into it.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:24.820
Because, sometimes, we have we have two varieties 
of language that are almost exactly the same.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.880
But they have two different names 
in two different countries, or...

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:32.180
They're spoken by two different ethnic groups 
and they both claim that language is their own.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:35.500
So they call it by different names
and it causes a lot of weird confusion.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.260
So I think it's like that in the former Yugoslavia 
for some of the languages there.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:47.200
There are people who just don't want to be associated
with the other people. So they called the language 
by the name of their own ethnic group.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.980
Even though, it's the same as the language 
of another ethnic group basically.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:57.500
So even though the standard written language might be 
pretty much the same, might be slightly different, 
but it's pretty much the same

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.440
and the spoken language is pretty much
 the same maybe with some variation, but...

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.200
they're mutually intelligible, even the standard 
written language is mutually intelligible,

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:09.960
they call it a different language 
because of whack ass politics

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:14.820
I don't like politics. I'm not a political person.
I'm more interested in cultures and in people.

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:19.480
So I don't really buy that definition. I don't like it when 
people say we speak a different language because...

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:22.580
we have a different ethnic group, a different nationality.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.600
We're going to call this language ours. 
It's not yours, it's ours.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.640
I don't really like that kind of territorial claiming 
of languages. So, I don't buy this definition

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:35.180
I think if you can speak together 
with each other and it's mutually intelligible,

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.940
you can read the same books then, in my opinion, 
you're speaking the same language.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:43.180
I don't really buy the political definition of
what's the language and what's a dialect.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:51.360
So basically my definition of a dialect is that it's 
mutually comprehensible with another variety of language.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:55.120
So, if you can communicate with them,
they understand you, you understand them

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:00.300
and you can use the written language elements of the
written language to make yourself even more understood,

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.380
then I think you are speaking dialects
of the same language.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:07.460
I hope that is helpful. I hope that give you
some... something to think about.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:12.200
Leave your comments down below. Tell me 
if you agree with my opinion on this matter.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:14.720
Alright. This is Paul... signing off.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:15.920
See ya...

